  "narrative": {
    "intro": "The Department of State obligated $5.5B on contracts in FY2026 to date. The Department of State made 18,116 awards with an average award size of $304K. Obligations fell 44.2% year over year. Obligations by fiscal year show a peak in FY22 at $11.9B, then a decline to $9.9B in FY25 and $5.5B in FY26. The Department of State concentrated FY2026 to date spending in security and IT categories, led by Security Guards and Patrol Services ($777M), Other Computer Related Services ($710M), and Computer Systems Design Services ($663M). The Department of State distributed FY2026 to date obligations across a mix of service and financial categories including Administrative Management and General Management Consulting Services ($429M) and Commercial Banking ($293M). The Department of State paid its largest vendors in FY2026 to date led by CGI FEDERAL INC. ($225M), CITIBANK, NATIONAL ASSOCIATION ($159M), and MISCELLANEOUS FOREIGN AWARDEES ($154M). Current-year totals are partial because FY2026 is fiscal year to date.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How has State's contract spending changed year over year?",
        "body": "The Department of State obligated $5.5B in FY2026 to date, down 44.2% year over year. The Department of State recorded obligations of $9.5B in FY21, $11.9B in FY22, $11.7B in FY23, $11.7B in FY24, and $9.9B in FY25. The Department of State made 18,116 awards in FY2026 to date with an average award size of $304K."
      },
      {
        "heading": "How concentrated is State's vendor and category spending?",
        "body": "Security Guards and Patrol Services is the largest category at $777M, followed by Other Computer Related Services at $710M and Computer Systems Design Services at $663M. CGI FEDERAL INC. is the largest vendor at $225M, followed by CITIBANK, NATIONAL ASSOCIATION at $159M and MISCELLANEOUS FOREIGN AWARDEES at $154M. The Department of State also shows notable FY2026 to date obligations to Administrative Management and General Management Consulting Services ($429M) and Commercial Banking ($293M)."
      }
    ]
  },
